#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:2
msgid "Migration Guides"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:4
msgid ""
"As a rule we avoid making breaking changes to poethepoet. However once in a "
"while it is deemed necessary to make some minor breaking changes, which may "
"impact a small minority of users, in order to make significant improvements "
"overall. This guide details instances when this has occurred and gives "
"advice on how to avoid or mitigate the impacts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:7
msgid "0.49.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:10
msgid "``sys.argv`` in script and expr tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:12
msgid ""
"Script tasks that call a function, and expr tasks, now get the "
"same :python:`sys.argv` as script tasks that run a module. If the task "
"declares args, :python:`sys.argv` holds them as parsed, with defaults "
"applied, followed by any args given after ``--``. For example, given:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:14
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"script = \"myapp:main()\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"port\", default = \"8000\" }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:20
msgid ""
"``poe serve -- --reload`` used to give ``sys.argv[1:] == [\"--\", \"--"
"reload\"]``, and now gives ``[\"--port\", \"8000\", \"--reload\"]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:22
msgid ""
"Also, ``${VAR}`` templates in arguments given on the command line are no "
"longer expanded for these tasks, matching other task types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:24
msgid ""
"See the :doc:`script task docs<../tasks/task_types/script>` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:27
msgid "Square bracket glob patterns in cmd tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:29
msgid ""
"Whitespace, backslashes, quotes, and parameter expansions inside a square "
"bracket pattern are now interpreted like in bash, instead of being taken "
"literally. To match a space or a backslash, escape it with a backslash, for "
"example ``[a\\ b]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:33
msgid "0.47.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:36
msgid "Boolean arg defaults must be boolean values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:38
msgid ""
"The ``default`` of an arg with :toml:`type = \"boolean\"` is now validated "
"when the config is loaded. It must be a TOML boolean, one of the case-"
"insensitive string literals ``\"t\"``, ``\"true\"``, ``\"1\"``, ``\"f\"``, "
"``\"false\"``, ``\"0\"``, or ``\"\"``, or a templated string such as ``\"$"
"{SOME_VAR}\"`` that resolves to one of those literals. Any other string is "
"rejected as a config error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:40
msgid ""
"From 0.43.0 through 0.46.0 an arbitrary string default was accepted and "
"exposed as the value of the variable when the flag was not provided, which "
"made it possible to switch between two strings using the ``:-`` operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:42
msgid ""
"# OLD: no longer supported\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.greet]\n"
"cmd = \"echo ${hello:-hello!}\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"hello\", type = \"boolean\", default = \"hi!\" }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:49
msgid ""
"As of 0.49.0 the same result is achieved with the ``true_string`` and "
"``false_string`` options, which set the string that a boolean arg is exposed "
"as when its value is true or false respectively. The arg can then be "
"referenced directly, without an expansion operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:51
msgid ""
"# NEW: requires 0.49.0 or later\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.greet]\n"
"cmd = 'echo \"${hello}\"'\n"
"args = [{ name = \"hello\", type = \"boolean\", true_string = \"hello!\", "
"false_string = \"hi!\" }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:58
msgid ""
"Note that the ``:-`` and ``:+`` operators test the resulting string rather "
"than the boolean value, so a nonempty ``false_string`` makes ``${hello:+...}"
"`` expand even when the flag is false. See the :doc:`args guide<../guides/"
"args_guide>` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:60
msgid ""
"These options were not available in 0.47.x and 0.48.0, where the workaround "
"is to make the arg a string type or move the logic into a shell or expr task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:64
msgid "0.46.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:66
msgid ""
"This release rewrites the envfile parser to align with standard dotenv "
"conventions and bash assignment syntax, and adds support for parameter "
"expansion in env file values. Two edge-case behaviours changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:69
msgid "Whitespace in unquoted values is now preserved"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:71
msgid ""
"Previously, whitespace terminated an unquoted value, making it possible to "
"write multiple assignments on a single line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:73
msgid ""
"# OLD: two assignments on one line (no longer supported)\n"
"FOO=bar BAZ=qux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:78
msgid ""
"This is now parsed as a single assignment ``FOO`` with the value ``bar "
"BAZ=qux``. Move such assignments to separate lines, or quote the value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:80 ../../../guides/migrations.rst:98
msgid ""
"# NEW: one assignment per line\n"
"FOO=bar\n"
"BAZ=qux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:87
msgid "Semicolons are no longer assignment separators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:89
msgid ""
"Previously, ``;`` acted as an assignment separator (similar to shell "
"statement separators), allowing constructs like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:91
msgid ""
"# OLD: two assignments separated by ; (no longer supported)\n"
"FOO=bar;BAZ=qux"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:96
msgid ""
"Semicolons are now treated as regular characters in unquoted values, so the "
"above assigns ``bar;BAZ=qux`` to ``FOO``. Move assignments to separate lines:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:104
msgid ""
"If you have a value that contains a literal semicolon, it now works without "
"escaping:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:106
msgid ""
"# NEW: semicolons in values need no special treatment\n"
"JDBC_URL=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb?options=first;second"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:113
msgid "0.44.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:115
msgid ""
"This release adds support for recursive includes, which allows included "
"files to themselves include other files. If a project includes config from "
"another file which in turn includes config from other files then these "
"transitive includes will now also be included in the main project by "
"default. This new behavior can be disabled by setting ``recursive = false`` "
"for a specific include, which will prevent any includes from that file from "
"being followed. For more details see the :doc:`include guide<../guides/"
"include_guide>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:117
msgid ""
"[tool.poe]\n"
" include = [{ path = \"external/tasks.toml\", recursive = false }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:122
msgid ""
"When ``recursive`` is ``false``, the included file's own tasks and "
"environment variables are still loaded, but any ``include`` entries within "
"that file are not followed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:126
msgid "0.43.0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:128
msgid ""
"This release included a major refactor of how variables are managed which "
"could unexpectedly change behavior in some situations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:131
msgid "Change in handling of boolean args"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:133
msgid ""
"To better support usage of boolean args in task logic, instead of mapping "
"the arg value ``false`` to the string value ``False`` in the corresponding "
"environment variable, it is instead mapped to *the environment variable "
"being unset*, even if it was previously set on the environment. This allows "
"more natural usage from most contexts, such as parameter expansion logic in "
"cmd or shell tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:135
msgid ""
"However some tasks or scripts may need to be updated if they previously "
"checked for ``\"False\"`` specifically, or if they use ``set -u``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:137
msgid ""
"Additionally if the variable was accessed from a python script "
"via :python:`os.environ[\"flag\"]` then this will break now. It is "
"recommended to instead use :python:`\"flag\" in os.environ` "
"or :python:`os.environ.get(\"flag\")` to check if the flag is set to true."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:139
msgid ""
"As of 0.49.0 the string value for each case can be configured per arg with "
"the ``true_string`` and ``false_string`` options. For example "
"setting :toml:`false_string = \"\"` results in the variable being set to an "
"empty string instead of unset when the flag is false, which avoids both of "
"the problems described above. See the :doc:`args guide<../guides/"
"args_guide>` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:141
msgid ""
"Note that as of this release you can reference the flag directly like a "
"local python variable with a bool value in expr or script tasks, even if the "
"arg was provided to a parent task, like a switch or sequence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:143
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.check-flag]\n"
"expr = \"flag and 'cli flag was set' or 'cli flag was not set'\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"flag\", type = \"boolean\"}]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:151
msgid "Introduction of private variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:153
msgid ""
"Any variable set within the project or task config (including referenced "
"envfiles) that contains no uppercase letters and starts with an underscore, "
"e.g. ``_private`` will not be exposed as an environment variable accessible "
"to the task at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:155
msgid ""
"It seems unlikely that anyone would specifically need to pass variables like "
"this to a task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:157
msgid ""
"Using private variables is now encouraged as a best practice to avoid "
"unintentionally setting environment variables on task subprocesses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:159
msgid ""
"Arg names are typically referenced within task content, and normally set as "
"environment variables. However if an arg name is prefixed with an underscore "
"to make it private, and there are no options explicitly configured for that "
"arg, then any leading underscores will be stripped from the name when "
"generating an option name from it. For example the arg name ``_flag`` will "
"result in the cli option ``--flag``. The same applies to positional args: a "
"private positional arg called ``_target`` will be displayed as ``target`` in "
"help, while still being treated as a private variable inside the task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/migrations.rst:161
msgid ""
"Since this can cause collisions between options, there is now a config "
"validation to prevent collisions between cli options."
msgstr ""
